    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that the math books remain together and the English books remain together, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as one unit and the English books as another unit:**\n   - Since all 3 math books must stay together and all 5 English books must stay together, we can treat each group as a single \"super book.\"\n   \n2. **Arrange the two \"super books\" on the shelf:**\n   - There are 2 \"super books\" (one for the math books and one for the English books), so there are \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways to arrange these two \"super books.\" This gives:\n     \\[\n     2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2 \\text{ ways}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each \"super book\":**\n   - The math books are all different, so there are \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways to arrange the 3 math books. This gives:\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6 \\text{ ways}\n     \\]\n   - Similarly, the English books are all different, so there are \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways to arrange the 5 English books. This gives:\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120 \\text{ ways}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - To find the total number of ways to arrange all the books, we multiply the number of ways to arrange the \"super books\" by the number of ways to arrange the books within each \"super book\":\n     \\[\n     2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
