    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We will treat the math books as one \"super book\" and the English books as another \"super book,\" for a total of 2 \"super books\". We would then have one \"super book\" for the math books and one for the English books in total, so we have 2 ways to arrange these \"super books\" since they are both different. The number of ways to arrange the contents of each individual \"super book\" is 3! and 5!, so the overall number of arrangements is the product of these 2 sets of arrangements. To solve the problem, we can consider the way to arrange the \"super books\". There are 2 possible arrangements of these \"super books\": when the math books come before the English books or when the English books come before the math books. Since we have 3 math books out of 6 books, 2 choose 3 is C(6, 3). For the math books' arrangement, it is 3!, and for the English books' arrangement, it is 5!. The total arrangements would be: $$2 \\times C(6, 3) \\times 3! \\times 5!$$\nReasoning:\nSince the math books must stay together and the English must stay together, they must be separated by other books.\nWe know there must be exactly 1 space between the 3 math books (and the remaining spaces will be filled by the 5 English books), so there are 4 spaces to fill (the spaces between the 3 math books) and then fill the rest of the space with the 5 English books.\nTo find the total number of ways, we need to determine how many ways we can choose to place the math books and the English books in these spaces.\nThere are 4 total spaces where the math books can go (in between the 3 math books), and since 3 math books are being placed, they can go into any of these 4 spaces.\nFor the English books, there are 5 spaces where they can go, and since 5 English books are being placed, they can go into any of these 5 spaces as well.\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the math books and English books is 4 x 5 = 20.\nHowever, since the math books are all different and the English books are all different, we also need to account for the permutations of the books. The number of permutations for the math books and English books is 3! (the number of ways to arrange the math books) and 5! (the number of ways to arrange the English books), respectively.\nMultiplying these together, we get 20 x (3!)(5!) = 20 x 6 x 120 = 14,400 ways to arrange the books.\nThe final answer is 14,400.",
